{% extends 'blog/post.html.twig' %}
{% block article %}
    <article id="blog-post">
        <h1>Flow PHP - Release 0.48.0</h1>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Flow 0.48.0 is out, and this one is mostly about three things: better visibility into what a pipeline is
            doing, memory and performance.
        </p>

        <h2>Flow Pulse Protocol</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Long running pipelines used to be a black box. You started one, and until it finished there was no good way
            to tell if it is sorting, joining, writing or just stuck.
        </p>
        <p>
            Now Flow can call you back while it works. A heartbeat is a callback registered through
            <code>config_builder()->heartbeat()</code>, and Flow calls it every N seconds while a run is busy.
            Each call gets a <code>Pulse</code> that lists every stage of the run, including the ones that are only
            planned, with their counters and the memory peak of the run.
        </p>
        <p>
            The most common case is a pipeline that holds a lock or a queue message. The lease on it expires after
            a few minutes, and a pipeline that runs longer loses it, even though it is perfectly healthy.
            With a heartbeat the pipeline extends the lease on every beat, so as long as it is alive, the lease stays.
        </p>
        <pre><code class="language-php" {{ stimulus_controller('syntax_highlight') }}>{% apply escape %}{% include template_folder ~ '/heartbeat.php' %}{% endapply %}</code></pre>
        <p>
            When the process dies, beats stop, the lease expires and something else can pick up the work.
            That is exactly how we can tell a slow pipeline from a dead one.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can read more about it in <a href="/documentation/components/core/heartbeat">documentation</a>.
        </p>
        <h3>Console progress</h3>
        <p>
            There is also a new package,
            <a href="https://github.com/flow-php/symfony-console-bridge" target="_blank"><code>flow-php/symfony-console-bridge</code></a>,
            that turns the pulse into a progress output for Symfony Console commands. It comes with four styles.
        </p>
        <pre><code class="language-php" {{ stimulus_controller('syntax_highlight') }}>{% apply escape %}{% include template_folder ~ '/console.php' %}{% endapply %}</code></pre>
        <p>
            Flow CLI file commands are already using it.
        </p>

        <h2>Pipe Operator</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            PHP 8.5 comes with a pipe operator, and Flow supports it. There is a pipeable DSL step for every
            DataFrame method, so a pipeline can be written without a single <code>-></code>.
        </p>
        <pre><code class="language-php" {{ stimulus_controller('syntax_highlight') }}>{% apply escape %}{% include template_folder ~ '/pipe.php' %}{% endapply %}</code></pre>
        <p>
            Both forms give the same result, use the one you like more.
            More details in <a href="/documentation/components/core/pipe-operator">documentation</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Parquet engine in flow_php</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Until now, reading and writing Parquet through a native code required arrow extension.
            In 0.48.0 the <code>flow_php</code> extension got its own Parquet engine, so one extension is enough.
        </p>
        <p>
            When <code>flow_php</code> is loaded, Parquet adapter picks <code>FlowPhpParquetEngine</code> by default,
            without it Flow falls back to the pure PHP engine. Nothing changes in the code of a pipeline.
        </p>
        <p>
            What is even more important, memory used by the engine is now counted by the memory limit of a run.
        </p>

        <h2>Memory</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            This is where a lot of our efforts were focused on in this release.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>Each operation (sort, group by, join) has its own memory budget, and a memory limit is shared by all
                workers of a run
            </li>
            <li>Memory limit counts only the memory an operation adds after it starts</li>
            <li>Without PHP <code>memory_limit</code> the default limit is 4 GiB</li>
            <li>A spilling group by was holding 2.5 times its memory limit, this is fixed</li>
            <li>On Linux, freed large buffers are returned to the system</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The results are easy to notice. Sorting 10 GB Parquet file now peaks at 9.3 GiB of memory, in 0.47.0 it was
            17.9 GiB.
        </p>

        <h2>Performance</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            Hash join now matches keys through the group table and it got native kernels for partitioning rows into
            buckets and for looking up keys. It also skips the pair check when equal keys already decide the condition.
        </p>
        <p>
            String functions <code>startsWith()</code>, <code>endsWith()</code> and <code>contains()</code> have native
            kernels too, and naive ISO datetimes are parsed natively.
        </p>

        <h2>Benchmarks</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            We have a new page, <a href="https://benchmarks.flow-php.com" target="_blank">benchmarks.flow-php.com</a>,
            where Flow is compared with DuckDB, Polars and Spark on 1, 5 and 10 GB datasets.
        </p>
        <figure>
            <a href="https://benchmarks.flow-php.com" target="_blank">
                <img src="{{ asset('images/blog/flow-php-release-0480/benchmarks.png') }}" alt="Flow 0.48.0 total time and peak memory compared with DuckDB, Polars and Spark on 10 GB">
            </a>
            <figcaption>Total time and peak memory of 16 benchmarks on 10 GB</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <p>
            Flow is not the fastest engine there, and it does not need to be. DuckDB and Polars are written in C++ and
            Rust.
            But the gap is much smaller than most people expect from PHP, and in some cases there is no gap at all.
        </p>
        <p>
            For example, converting 10 GB of CSV to JSON takes Flow 10 seconds, DuckDB and Polars need 12 seconds and
            Spark 36 seconds.
            Flow needs 0.5 GiB of memory for it.
        </p>
        <p>
            On that page you can switch between Flow with and without the extension, and between 0.47.0 and 0.48.0.
            We are going to add each new release there, so the progress will be visible.
        </p>

        <h2>Other changes</h2>
        <hr/>
        <ul>
            <li>PostgreSQL: session settings (like <code>statement_timeout</code>) per connection, also in Symfony
                PostgreSQL Bundle
            </li>
            <li>PostgreSQL: <code>ALTER EXTENSION ... SET SCHEMA</code> in the query builder, schema diff moves
                extensions between schemas
            </li>
            <li>S3 and Azure filesystems: http timeouts and retries, <code>mv()</code> of objects above 5 GB, safer
                <code>append</code></li>
            <li>Loaders name the table or index they write to, visible in telemetry</li>
            <li>Playground: type-aware autocompletion, hover docs, parameter hints and automatic imports</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Heads up</h2>
        <hr/>
        <p>
            There are a few API changes in this release:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>Transformation::transform()</code> was renamed to <code>__invoke()</code></li>
            <li><code>print_rows(Rows)</code> was renamed to <code>format_rows()</code></li>
            <li><code>MemoryLimit</code> moved to <code>Flow\ETL\Values\Memory</code></li>
            <li><code>DataFrame::with()</code> accepts only <code>Transformer</code> or <code>Transformation</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Please check the <a href="/documentation/upgrading">upgrade guide</a> before updating.
        </p>
        <p>
            Those are just the most important additions to Flow PHP. For more details, check full at
            <a href="/changelog">Changelog</a>
        </p>
    </article>
{% endblock %}
